'''II.''' ''n.'' '''1.''' A monk or nun belonging to one of the religious congregations following the rule of St. Basil. {{smaller|These comprise nearly all the Greek and Oriental monasteries, and are found in communion with Rome in Sicily, and in the Greco-Ruthenian and Armenian rites. Several Basilian monasteries in Spain were suppressed in 1835.}} '''2.''' One of a congregation of priests devoted to the education of young men for the priesthood, founded in 1800 by the archbishop of Vienne, France. {{smaller|Their name is derived from their first house, in the parish of St. Basil in Vivarais. They have establishments in France, England, and Africa.}}

'''basilic''' (ba-sil′ik), ''a.'' and ''n.'' [Formerly also ''basilick'', < F. ''basilique'', < L. ''basilicus'', < Gr. βασιλικός, kingly, royal, < βασιλεύς, king.] '''I.''' ''a.'' '''1.''' Kingly; royal.—'''2.''' Of or pertaining to a basilica; basilican.{{smaller|—'''Basilic vein,''' the largest of the veins of the arm, formed by the junction of two ulnar veins with the median basilic vein, piercing the deep fascia a little above the elbow on the inner side of the arm, ascending in the course of the brachial artery, and ending in the axillary vein, before or after receiving the venæ comites of the brachial artery.—'''Median basilic vein,''' a short venous trunk at the bend of the elbow, crossing the track of the brachial artery, from which it is separated by the bicipital fascia, and terminating in the basilic.}}

Also ''basilical.''

'''II.''' ''n.'' '''1.''' A basilica (which see).—'''2.''' In ''anat.'', the basilic vein. See I.

