'''Basilica<sup>2</sup>''' (ba-sil′i-kä̤), ''n. pl.'', also used as ''sing.'' [ML., < LGr. βασιλικά, neut. pl. of Gr. βασιλικός, royal (or, less prob., relating to Basil I.).] A code of laws of the Byzantine empire, adapted from the laws of Justinian in the ninth century, by order of the emperor Basil I. Also ''Basilics.''

'''basilical''' (ba-sil′i-ka̤l), ''a.'' [< ''basilica''<sup>1</sup> + ''-al.''] Same as ''basilic.''

'''basilican''' (ba-sil′i-ka̤n), ''a.'' [< ML. ''basilicanus'', < L. ''basilica'', basilica.] Pertaining to or resembling a basilica; basilic.—'''Basilican ointment.''' {{smaller|Same as ''basilicon''.}}

readings.c2s2:
